{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210732,"nonce":"BLGmyBii7/InbiyNvwlemPnLusg=","title":"AOT SM clone block-param collision: per-clone fresh vids + clone_rename on yield_saves","message":"Fixed in commit 59ea852. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\n**RESOLVED at HEAD `59ea852`.** Per-clone-region fresh ValueId allocation: each non-primary clone gets unique vids for its params + instruction defs, indexed by `(orig_vid, subs_key)` so cross-block references within the same clone region all resolve to each other's fresh vids.\n\n\nIn Phase 3 of `transform_to_state_machine` (`src/codegen/aot_state_machine.rs`), cloned blocks were populated like:\n\n```rust\nblk.params = params.clone();         // SAME ValueIds as original\nblk.instructions = clone_insts;      // SAME def vids as original\n```\n\nThe original block and its clone both declared e.g. `v14, v15, v16` as block params. The cranelift backend's `val_map: HashMap\u003cMirValueId, CraneliftValue\u003e` is single-binding — whichever block was lowered last overwrote the first's entry for those vids.\n\nWhen a block downstream of the ORIGINAL referenced `v14`, the lookup gave the CLONE's param Value, which wasn't dominated on the original-region path. Cranelift emitted IR that took the wrong path or simply read garbage from a register that was alive only on the clone path.\n\nSurfaced as `Reader.readLine` hanging on the SECOND call (when `_lineBuf` had leftover bytes from the first call). The inner search loop's `i` param defined by both `bb10` (original) and `bb49` (clone) made val_map flip-flop; the back-edge `jump bb10(i+1, ...)` materialised an arg pointing at the wrong block's param Value, and at runtime the loop saw stale data and never found the newline.\n\n\nBuild a `clone_rename: HashMap\u003c(ValueId, SubsKey), ValueId\u003e` keyed by both the original vid AND the clone's subs_key:\n\n- For each non-primary clone of block `B`, allocate a fresh `ValueId` for every block param and instruction def in `B`. Store it under `(orig_vid, this_clone's_subs_key)`.\n- When populating a clone's body, build a `combined` rename: the reaching subs (yield-load renames) UNION every entry in `clone_rename` keyed by THIS clone's subs_key.\n  - This covers the clone's own params/defs.\n  - It also covers UPSTREAM clones in the same clone region (= clones of other blocks reached on the same path, identified by the same subs_key). Cross-block references inside the cloned region all resolve to each other's fresh vids.\n- Primary clones (= the original block) keep their original vids. Their bodies are reached on paths where the original's val_map binding is correct.\n\n\n| Spec | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `aot_runtime` | 21/21 | 21/21 |\n| `e2e` | 113/113 (parallel flakes) | 113/113 |\n| `events.spec` | 36/36 | 36/36 |\n| `proc.spec` | 32/33 | 31/33 |\n| `http.spec` | 24/25 | 24/25 |\n| `io.spec` | crashes at 26/44 | **44/44** |\n| `BufferReader.readLine` | hangs on 2nd call | works |\n\n**Net gain:** io.spec went from broken (crashing mid-suite) to fully passing. BufferReader.readLine pattern works. One proc.spec regression (\"two fibers reading different processes interleave\") — separate root cause in the proc-pipe yield path, not the SM transform per se. Documented as follow-up below.\n\n\nAfter the fresh-vid allocation fix, a separate bug surfaced when a function had **two yielding call sites** to the same method, e.g. `var a = prod.next; ... while (true) { var h = prod.next; ... }`. The second call returned null without entering the callee's body, AND the parent's loop locals got corrupted to null on resume.\n\n\n`build_layout` was applying ONLY the resume-load subs (`subs.get(vid)`) to `yield_saves` operands. It did NOT apply the per-clone `clone_rename` table. For a clone of the yielding block reached on path P, the saved live values (e.g. bb5's params v22..v26) had been renamed to v125..v129 in that path's cloned loop header (bb21). But `yield_saves[clone_bid]` still listed v22..v26 unchanged. At lowering, `val_map[v22]` resolved to bb5's PRIMARY param Value (a different path), and the save sequence wrote garbage. On resume, the loads bound v82..v86 to that garbage. Surfaced as `iter null` and `h null` on second-call resume in proc.spec / @hatch:web `App.serve_` shapes.\n\n\nIn `src/codegen/aot_state_machine.rs`:\n1. Returned `clone_rename: HashMap\u003c(ValueId, SubsKey), ValueId\u003e` from `clone_per_reaching_subs`.\n2. Plumbed it into `build_layout`.\n3. Added a `resolve(vid, subs, key)` helper that tries `subs.get(vid)` first, then `clone_rename.get((vid, key))`, else returns the vid unchanged.\n4. Applied `resolve` to `yield_saves` entries AND to `BlockKind::CrossFnCallInit { receiver, args }`.\n\n\n```wren\nclass Producer {\n  next {\n    Fiber.yield()\n    return \"value\"\n  }\n}\nclass Consumer {\n  static run(prod) {\n    var a = prod.next                  // call 1 — works\n    var iter = 0\n    while (true) {\n      var h = prod.next                // call 2 — used to return null, iter became null too\n      if (h == \"STOP\") break\n      iter = iter + 1\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\n`hatch/site` still hangs under AOT even after the fix. `/healthz` (a no-yield route) doesn't respond. Hangs ALSO under `WLIFT_USE_KRIO_FIBERS=1` (SM disabled), so it's NOT an SM issue. Likely a background-fiber that spins without yielding (one of `Catalog.refreshLoop` / `Api.warmAll` / `Catalog.warmReadmes` / `warmBlogs`), starving `sched.tick`. Catalog also reports `count = null` because `wlift_sqlite` doesn't load from the site binary's working dir (`DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` workaround required). Both are downstream issues, distinct from the SM transform.","files":null}]}